Mesa 26.2.6 for the Radeon RX 550 on big-endian PowerPC (Power Mac G5)
=======================================================================

These packages replace the Debian/MintPPC Mesa 26.2.6-1 packages of the same
names. They are built from Mesa 26.2.6 with the Debian patches and the same
drivers and options as the Debian ppc64 build, plus fixes that let the AMD
drivers run on a big-endian CPU:

  mesa-libgallium      Gallium drivers (radeonsi, r300, r600, nouveau, virgl,
                       zink, llvmpipe, softpipe), GL dispatch, VA-API
  libgl1-mesa-dri      DRI loader stubs and libdril
  libegl-mesa0         EGL (X11, Wayland, GBM, surfaceless)
  libglx-mesa0         GLX
  libgbm1              GBM
  mesa-va-drivers      empty, as in Debian (VA-API is in mesa-libgallium)
  mesa-vdpau-drivers   empty: Mesa removed VDPAU in 25.3 (use VA-API).
                       Installing it removes the Mesa 24 VDPAU drivers.
  mesa-vulkan-drivers  Vulkan (radv, lavapipe, venus, gfxstream, layers)
  mesa-opencl-icd      OpenCL 3.1 (Rusticl) on radeonsi. Debian builds this
                       package for ppc64el but not for ppc64; this one follows
                       the ppc64el package.

They use the Debian libllvm22 package; mesa-opencl-icd also needs libclc-22,
libclang-cpp22, libllvmspirvlib22.1 and an ICD loader (ocl-icd-libopencl1),
all from debian-ports.

OpenCL notes
------------
* Rusticl is enabled on radeonsi by default here (upstream and Debian need
  RUSTICL_ENABLE=radeonsi). clinfo shows "rusticl" with the RX 550.
* The device reports itself as big-endian (CL_DEVICE_ENDIAN_LITTLE = false):
  buffers, kernel arguments and images hold host-order data, as OpenCL
  requires. Atomics on buffers run as compare-and-swap loops.
* fp64 (cl_khr_fp64) is off by default, as upstream; RUSTICL_FEATURES=fp64
  turns it on.
* The first build of a program takes about 10 seconds on a PPC970MP (clang and
  libclc); later builds of the same source come from the shader cache in about
  a second. Driver updates empty that cache.

What changed compared with stock Mesa 26.2.6
--------------------------------------------
The full source diff is rx550-g5-vs-mesa-26.2.6.diff.gz in this directory
(it includes the three Debian patches). Almost all of it only takes effect on
a big-endian CPU with a GFX8 GPU (Polaris: RX 460/470/480/550/560/570/580);
other GPUs and CPUs get stock code.

* GPU commands: command buffers, fences, shader binaries, descriptors and
  query results are converted to the GPU's little-endian byte order. UVD
  decode messages are encoded little-endian.
* Application data: vertex and index buffers, texel and image buffers, SSBOs,
  buffer device addresses, clears and copies between buffers and images are
  converted in the driver or in the shader.
* On big-endian, radeonsi compiles shaders with LLVM; RADV uses ACO.
* Shared Mesa code that was wrong on big-endian: HTILE (depth compression)
  bitfields, packed-float formats, DRI/EGL framebuffer formats, Vulkan vertex
  binding strides, the sparse-buffer PRT workaround bit read from libdrm, and
  VA-API P010/P016 images (kept little-endian, as VA-API defines them).
* RADV reports Vulkan 1.3 on this configuration (the tested CTS is 1.3).
* OpenCL: SPIR-V and clang output on a big-endian host, libclc NIR
  serialisation, kernel memory byte order. Two fixes are not big-endian
  specific: scratch (private memory) accesses now carry their real alignment
  in the LLVM backend, and image copies on compute-only contexts discard DCC
  first so that float NaN payloads are copied bit for bit.

Tested on a Power Mac G5 (PPC970MP) with an RX 550, on the exact bytes of
these packages: Khronos GL CTS (GLES2/3/31 and KHR-GL46 lists, 17,887 cases)
and a stratified Vulkan CTS sample (63,560 cases) with no regressions against
the previous release; VA-API decode and VCE encode (H.264, HEVC) with ffmpeg;
the Khronos OpenCL CTS quick list (results below). This is not a Khronos
conformance submission.

